# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editors
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Agent tooling — per-developer session state and caches, not project content.
# (oh-my-claudecode writes session logs, a HUD cache, and project memory here;
# these mutate on every run and are personal to whoever is working.)
.omc/

# Machine-local Plot state. The rest of `.plot` is project content (briefs,
# templates, the review hold) and stays committed; this holds caches describing
# THIS machine's refs and worktrees — the board's last-good pulse among them.
# A checked-in pulse would be one clone telling another what its branches are
# doing.
.plot/state/
# The same cache, but written into a TEST FIXTURE by a browser test that starts a
# real board against it (`test/fixtures/*/.plot/state/`). The unanchored `.plot/
# state/` above only matches the repo root's own; a slash in the pattern anchors
# it there. This catches the nested ones every fixture produces on a test run.
**/.plot/state/

# The agent registry — one manifest per dispatched worker, and machine-local for
# the same reason the pulse above is. Each holds a `pid` and an ABSOLUTE
# `worktree` path (`/Users/.../plot-wt-<branch>`): facts true only on the machine
# that wrote them, and in another clone either meaningless or — worse — a pid
# that matches some unrelated process.
#
# Measured 2026-08-24: 54 manifests were on main and 48 of those were dead, one
# per agent ever dispatched here. Nothing reaps a tracked file, so the directory
# had become a graveyard shared with everyone who clones rather than a register
# of what is running.
#
# THE REGISTRY STILL WORKS. The board reads the DIRECTORY, never git — untracking
# changes what is shared, not what is recorded.
.plot/agents/

# A dispatched worker's own traces, written into ITS worktree by
# `plot-dispatch.sh`: the pid it started, the exit code it ended with, and the
# console log in between. Machine-local for the same reason as `.plot/state/` —
# a checked-in pid describes a process on one laptop.
#
# Untracked and unignored is the worst of both: they show up in `git status`,
# and a `git add -A` in a worker's worktree carries someone's console log into
# the repo. Caught once, before it landed.
.plot-worker.pid
.plot-worker.wrapper.pid
.plot-worker.exit
.plot-worker.log
